---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
// To use your own character, change only this path to "../components/Creature.astro".
import PetCreature from "../components/CreatureExample.astro";

// Start with the tasks on the page, then find the browser JavaScript below.
// Beginner JavaScript guide: docs/virtual-pet-javascript.md
// The finished styles are in src/styles/virtual-pet.css.
---

<BaseLayout title="Virtual Pet" description="Learn JavaScript by caring for your own virtual pet.">
    <div class="virtual-pet-project">
        <a href="/">Back to home</a>
        <header>
            <h1>Virtual Pet</h1>
            <p>Give your pet a personality and learn how JavaScript makes a page respond.</p>
            <p class="pet-learning-goal">Click a button → run a function → change a variable → update the page.</p>
        </header>

        <section class="pet-card" aria-labelledby="pet-name">
            <!-- The wrapper sizes either creature to fit the pet card. -->
            <div class="creature-bounds pet-creature" aria-hidden="true">
                <PetCreature />
            </div>
            <!-- Change your pet's name here. -->
            <h2 id="pet-name">Barnacle</h2>
            <p class="pet-happiness">Happiness: <span id="happiness-value">5</span> / 10</p>
            <p id="pet-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">Ready for a snack and some company.</p>

            <div class="pet-actions">
                <button id="feed-button" type="button">Feed</button>
                <button id="play-button" type="button" aria-describedby="unfinished-note">Play <small>Student task</small></button>
                <button id="rest-button" type="button" aria-describedby="unfinished-note">Rest <small>Student task</small></button>
            </div>
            <p id="unfinished-note">Feed works already. Play previews an animation; its happiness changes and the Rest action are your tasks.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="pet-tasks" aria-labelledby="tasks-heading">
            <h2 id="tasks-heading">Your tasks</h2>
            <p>Need help with JavaScript? Open <code>docs/virtual-pet-javascript.md</code> in your editor for explanations, examples, and debugging tips.</p>
            <ol>
                <li>Try the Feed button.</li>
                <li>Find its function and event listener in the code.</li>
                <li>Change the pet’s name and feeding message, then swap in your own creature.</li>
                <li>Make Play increase happiness by 2 and show a different message.</li>
                <li>Make Rest show a resting message without changing happiness.</li>
                <li>Ensure Play cannot increase happiness above 10.</li>
                <li>Add a timer with <code>setInterval</code> that lowers happiness by 1 every 5 seconds and updates the page.</li>
                <li>When happiness reaches 0, end the game: stop the timer, show a game-over message, disable the care buttons, and fade out the creature.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>Open <code>src/pages/virtual-pet.astro</code>. Change the name in the HTML, then find the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> below it for the JavaScript. Choose your own messages and personality for your pet.</p>
            <p>Start with the example character. When your own design is ready, change the <code>PetCreature</code> import path at the top of this file from <code>../components/CreatureExample.astro</code> to <code>../components/Creature.astro</code>. Keep <code>&lt;PetCreature /&gt;</code> in place—the wrapper will size your character to fit.</p>
            <p>Test one change at a time. Try Play when happiness is 9, and check that Rest leaves the number unchanged.</p>

            <h3>Ready-made animations</h3>
            <p>Feed gives the creature a little munching squash, Play makes it hop and wiggle, and Rest gives it a slow breathing motion. Feed and Play are connected already. The animations only change how the creature looks.</p>
            <p>Use <code>animatePet("is-resting")</code> inside your Rest function. The other choices are <code>animatePet("is-feeding")</code> and <code>animatePet("is-playing")</code>. Each helper call adds a CSS class, plays its animation once, and can be repeated. Add your Play logic to the existing <code>playPet()</code> function.</p>

            <h3>Make it a survival game</h3>
            <p>The timer is your task to build. <code>setInterval</code> repeats a function after a delay measured in milliseconds: 5000 means 5 seconds. Start one timer when the page loads and save its ID so you can stop it with <code>clearInterval</code>. Don’t start a new timer on every button click.</p>
            <p>Keep happiness between 0 and 10. After each change, check whether the game is over, then call <code>updatePet()</code>. Use a variable to remember that the game has ended so button functions cannot change the values or replace the game-over message.</p>
            <p>The fade CSS is ready: select <code>.pet-creature</code> and use <code>classList.add</code> to add the class <code>is-game-over</code>. Set each care button’s <code>disabled</code> property to <code>true</code>. Leave the final happiness and message visible.</p>
            <p>Test by waiting without feeding: happiness should reach 0, stay there, and the creature should fade out. The buttons should stop working. Refresh the page to start again.</p>

            <h3>Optional challenges</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Change the creature’s colour or expression depending on its happiness.</li>
                <li>Add an energy variable: playing uses energy and resting restores it. Lower energy in your existing timer too. When any implemented attribute reaches 0, end the game—even if a button caused it.</li>
                <li>Prevent playing when energy is too low, with a helpful message.</li>
                <li>Add a reset button to restore the starting values, show the creature, and enable the care buttons. Stop the old timer before starting one new timer.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
    </div>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
    // This script runs in the browser, where it can respond to button clicks.
    // Select HTML elements by their IDs so we can read or change them in JavaScript.
    const happinessDisplay = document.querySelector("#happiness-value");
    const statusDisplay = document.querySelector("#pet-status");
    const feedButton = document.querySelector("#feed-button");
    const playButton = document.querySelector("#play-button");
    const petCreature = document.querySelector(".pet-creature");

    // Ready-made visual helper: restart the animation even after a quick repeat click.
    function animatePet(animationClass) {
        if (petCreature.classList.contains("is-game-over")) return;
        petCreature.classList.remove("is-feeding", "is-playing", "is-resting");
        // Let the browser notice the removed class before adding it again.
        petCreature.getBoundingClientRect();
        petCreature.classList.add(animationClass);
    }

    petCreature.addEventListener("animationend", (event) => {
        // Ignore animations on features inside the creature.
        if (event.target !== petCreature) return;
        petCreature.classList.remove("is-feeding", "is-playing", "is-resting");
    });

    // `let` lets us change these values as the pet is cared for.
    let happiness = 5;
    let statusMessage = "Ready for a snack and some company.";

    function updatePet() {
        // Changing a variable does not change the HTML automatically.
        // textContent puts our latest values into the page as text.
        happinessDisplay.textContent = String(happiness);
        statusDisplay.textContent = statusMessage;
    }

    function feedPet() {
        animatePet("is-feeding");
        if (happiness < 10) {
            happiness = happiness + 1;
            statusMessage = "Yum! That snack made my day.";
        }

        if (happiness === 10) {
            statusMessage = "I’m already happy at 10 out of 10. Thanks for caring for me!";
        }

        updatePet();
    }

    // An event listener runs our named function when the button is clicked.
    feedButton.addEventListener("click", feedPet);

    function playPet() {
        animatePet("is-playing");
        // TODO: Increase happiness by 2, choose a message, and call updatePet().
        // Make sure happiness cannot go above 10, even when it starts at 9.
    }

    playButton.addEventListener("click", playPet);

    // TODO: Rest
    // Select #rest-button, write a named function, and attach a click listener.
    // Change the message without changing happiness, then call updatePet().
    // Call animatePet("is-resting") to use the ready-made breathing animation.

    // TODO: Decay over time
    // Write a named function that lowers happiness by 1 without going below 0.
    // Use setInterval to run it every 5000 milliseconds; save the timer ID.
    // Start the timer once, not inside updatePet() or a button function.
    // Check for game over after changing a value, then update the page.
    // If you add energy or other attributes, lower them in this same timer.

    // TODO: Game over
    // Track whether the game has ended with a variable.
    // Check after timer AND button changes: any implemented attribute at 0 ends it.
    // Use clearInterval to stop the timer and set a game-over status message.
    // Add is-game-over to .pet-creature to use the fade styling.
    // Disable the care buttons and prevent their functions changing an ended game.

    // Show the starting values when the page first loads.
    updatePet();
</script>
